export const LEARN_MORE_ROOM_SCRIPTS = {"room-01": "One tune can have more than one name. Twinkle Twinkle Little Star and The ABC Song use the same tune. Sing either set of words, and the notes take the same path.", "room-02": "A part that comes back makes a song easy to join. Old MacDonald brings back the farm sounds. BINGO brings back the dog's name.", "room-03": "A steady beat can guide your feet. Skip to My Lou gives you dance steps. When the Saints Go Marching In gives you march steps.", "room-04": "A song can make you think of someone far away. In My Bonnie Lies over the Ocean, the singer asks for Bonnie to come back. The words make the tune feel like a song about missing someone.", "room-05": "People in different countries sing different songs about flowers. Sakura Sakura sings of cherry blooms in Japan. Mo Li Hua sings of jasmine in China.", "room-06": "Songs from nearby places can have their own tunes. Rasa Sayang comes from the Malay world. Lao Duang Duen comes from Thailand, and its tune sounds different.", "room-07": "A song can move from place to place and change as people play it. Misirlou has been played by many kinds of bands. Each band can keep the tune and change the sound.", "room-08": "A tune can stay while its words change. The tune from Shchedryk was given new English words in Carol of the Bells. Listen for the same notes in both songs.", "room-09": "A song can make people think of a place. El Cóndor Pasa was written in Peru, and its title means The Condor Passes. The tune can bring Peru to mind.", "room-10": "Some songs tell us what is being marked. Happy Birthday to You marks a birthday. Auld Lang Syne is sung as one year ends and a new one starts.", "room-11": "Winter songs can begin in very different ways. Silent Night was made as a carol. Deck the Hall uses an old Welsh tune called Nos Galan.", "room-12": "A low part can repeat while the rest of the music moves. In Pachelbel's Canon in D, the same low notes come back again and again. New lines grow above them.", "room-13": "Instruments can make you picture the weather. In Vivaldi's Spring, bright notes come back like bird calls. In his Summer Storm, quick notes rush like wind and rain.", "room-14": "A tune can come back with parts changed. In Mozart's Twinkle set, the same tune gets new notes around it each time. You can still hear Twinkle inside.", "room-15": "A few notes can grow into a big piece. Beethoven's Fifth Symphony starts with four short notes. He brings those notes back and builds more music from them.", "room-16": "An instrument can sound as if it sings, even with no words. In Mendelssohn's Violin Concerto, the violin plays a long, smooth tune. The rest of the orchestra plays below it.", "room-17": "One note can paint a picture when it comes back. In Chopin's Raindrop Prelude, one note taps again and again. It can make you think of drops of rain.", "room-18": "Dance beats can move from the dance floor into a big music hall. In Dvořák's Slavonic Dance, the orchestra plays strong dance beats. You can hear music made to move.", "room-19": "Ballet music helps dancers know how to move. The Swan Lake tune glides in long lines. The Russian Dance races ahead for quick steps.", "room-20": "Music can make a creature appear in your mind. In The Swan, the cello plays one long, smooth tune. The tune moves like a swan on water.", "room-21": "Fast notes can make you picture fast flight. In Flight of the Bumblebee, the notes race up and down. They sound like a bee that will not sit still.", "room-22": "Music can help a show race to its end. In the William Tell finale, quick notes gallop like horses. The fast beat tells your ears that the end is near.", "room-23": "Quiet gaps can be part of the music. In Satie's First Gymnopédie, slow notes have room to fade. The gaps help the piece feel calm and still.", "room-24": "Two pieces in this room sound very different. Joplin's Maple Leaf Rag has a bouncy piano beat. Holst's Jupiter uses a large orchestra for a wide, bold sound."};
